 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6585fdc9-9c5f-5608-81ab-8834986c15c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126755 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cec1597b-145a-5f80-bebd-a8347c83ce22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126756 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{12}{11}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126756 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126756 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a840cea9-875d-5806-9749-b1cad3c12497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126757 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{16}{11}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126757 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126757 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03f4681f-e4aa-5573-8bfc-d6735a168c93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126758 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e05d6e9-5f4b-5d3e-b3e2-e0c53b441921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126759 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{18}{11}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126759 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126759 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3399dbf2-0d3b-5140-b468-f8c7977c9def" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126760 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39da1e78-3bd7-5538-9c64-88552580b68f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128866 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128866 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses $m_1=1\\,\\textrm{kg},$ $m_2=2\\,\\textrm{kg},$ $m_3=3\\,\\textrm{kg},$ and $m_4=3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ located at $x_1=-1\\,\\textrm{m},$ $x_2 = 1\\,\\textrm{m},$ $x_3 = 3\\,\\textrm{m},$ and $x_4 = 5 \\, \\textrm{m},$ respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128866 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82248f5b-84f1-5acf-ade8-4ed90a410cc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128866 #edb/ref 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\nM_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =1, & \\,{m}_{2} & =2, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, & \\,{m}_{4} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =1, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3, & \\,{x}_{4} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-1+2+9+15 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $25\\,\\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128867 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128867 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/cea41dc5efa961e4d0e3ccf42db21113d3b7d1d2240f3edbcc4c1f0cab61ead1.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses above, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adcc17f7-7acf-50fd-bb68-b9e6b0477fe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128867 #edb/ref 1074 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overline{x},$ of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+8+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128868 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128868 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0e/0e4802c6642fc0950358235f30fb8c994d26b912e557122af08b99277bb6192a.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overline{y}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128868 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f13ad0c-9523-542d-a3bf-c562f09d6a88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128868 #edb/ref 1074 "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9+0+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128869 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128869 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/440f58e3d5e652374689083ffa672f505888259a51e1c5ef2207d94410eb8cab.png)\n\n     \n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $10\\,\\rm{lb}$ and is $4\\,\\rm{ft}$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128869 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae5cebdd-4eb8-5eed-9b89-fffb153c844b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128869 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A.$\nThe length of the rod is $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,\\rm{ft}.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n9 - 4 = 5\\,\\rm{ft},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc2503e1ed1cd91a9f3876ec5735d5c375c36b9bcac6216659a29f5efa294ea0.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m \\cdot 5 = -5m\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $10 \\cdot 4 = 40\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40-5m & =0 \\\\ 5m & =40 \\\\ m & =8\\,lb.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1039 "Sums of Moments for Masses Distributed Along the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129291 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b7ea6678-6624-5853-b137-be255a3e694e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Center of Mass for a Collection of Masses Along the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129292 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9af52eb2-33a4-5655-9b1a-a07d76dc3216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Center of Mass for a Collection of Masses in the Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129293 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "37fbfd74-981e-5281-a651-21b9ea1b7421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Principle of Moments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129294 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c97f4331-e2cf-5ec7-a3ea-3f03a4c60d2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1045 "Moments and Center of Mass" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129368 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e9ffd5e1-7cc1-5226-a079-e661d2db31dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129539 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses $m_{1} = 0.5kg$, $m_{2} = 2kg$, and $m_{3} = 1kg$ located at $x_{1} = 2m$, $x_{2} = 3m$, and $x_{3} = 5m$, respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1371e8b7-9ee1-5f72-8099-0b1ba9daf98c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129540 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/092a8fdc34bd72bb15f1b2ad68467232bafd5423b5662200d021eca3f249005f.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above, where the distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e68bef7-b75c-5baa-8bf8-48acf922e76c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129541 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses $m_{1} = 2kg$, $m_{2} = 5kg$, and $m_{3} = 7kg$ located at $x_{1} = 1.5$, $x_{2} = 5$, and $x_{3} = 10$, respectively, along the $x$-axis, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "253b036a-b515-5131-9744-15f0a018f8f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129543 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/467a702d26a9e8c088a74aca889e2adbc68c8f86192b7138b37bdbdff09a7582.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above, where the distances along the $x$ and $y$ axes are measured in meters. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(x^(―), y^(―))$, what is the value of $x^(―)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e37911d-be6b-5398-8c11-baa6836866a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130253 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130253 #edb/ref 1061 45153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130253 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22ddc670-2143-5ed3-9d9b-4b54b784c699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130253 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130254 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130254 #edb/ref 1061 45154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130254 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "778a833f-adf0-5d3b-a850-7ce9475c9064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130254 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130255 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130255 #edb/ref 1061 45155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130255 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46135edf-87fd-5493-b39a-4ae7024a6be9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130255 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130256 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130256 #edb/ref 1061 45156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130256 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9cefcc39-b448-5c22-88e0-379624bb627c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130256 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130257 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130257 #edb/ref 1061 45159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130257 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32f039c5-8981-589b-a3e9-3ca867a29f4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130257 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130258 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130258 #edb/ref 1061 45160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130258 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8dfcc42-33e6-588f-9789-192a1a8ef919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130258 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130259 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130259 #edb/ref 1061 45163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130259 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a999288a-56a3-5c23-a010-a76179a45113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130259 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130260 #edb/ref 1061 45164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130260 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7eda996b-9a98-5746-a841-a4b1120967f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130260 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130261 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130261 #edb/ref 1061 45167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130261 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47daef2d-4568-5d5c-b00f-6fa8530a724d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130261 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130475 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have two point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $20\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and $40\\,\\textrm{kg},$ respectively, connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0948ebde55c4a5f8819f741e70a44c31fca75218a65d1f88d503367f213c9e31.png)\n\nOur intuition tells us that if we place a **fulcrum** (i.e., a balancing point) at the *midpoint* between the two masses, the rod will tip down at the right end, as shown below.\n\nHow do we find their center of mass, [math]\\overline{x}?[/math]\n\n![](images/93/93e4ca7726e4c01793440e23823622d19ec284104e5a062af40a6b35407d410d.png)\n\nA typical example is a child's seesaw. If one child sits at each end of the seesaw with the fulcrum halfway between, the heavier child falls, and the lighter child rises.\n\nTo balance the rod perfectly, we must move the fulcrum closer to the larger mass. \n\nThe center of mass [math]\\overline{x}[/math] is where the fulcrum should be to balance the seesaw.\n\n![](images/00/00fb578163a19566869582929dfe9dc351ed3b75c44221f8d3e68157f40f08f7.png)\n\nAt what distance from the first mass ($d_1$ in the picture) should the fulcrum be placed for the masses to balance perfectly?\n\nTo answer this, we must introduce the concept of a **moment**:\n\n> *The moment of a mass about a point $P$ equals the product of the mass and its displacement from $P.$*\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Loosely speaking, moment describes the amount of *turning effect* produced by a mass about a point (usually the tip of the fulcrum).\n\n- For masses measured in kilograms and distances in meters, the units of a moment are $\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm m.$\n\n- Moment is a *vector* quantity. A moment is either *positive* (representing *clockwise* induced motion) or *negative* (for *counterclockwise* motion). A moment's sign (positive/clockwise or negative/counterclockwise) is determined by the direction the rod would rotate if the corresponding mass were the only mass in the system.\n\nLet's now see how to use moments to calculate the required distance $d_1.$\n\n* Imagine you're tightening a nut with a spanner. It's possible to produce the same turning effect with a long spanner of smaller mass as with a shorter planner with a more heavy mass." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130475 #edb/ref 1065 7804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130475 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3399bc78-596d-52d5-abe0-6460588f0b59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130475 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130476 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/e6/e6060d68d4f0952ad5e42128697461c14425bb4f7bb4e124e0253919e38b9561.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass *about the fulcrum*:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-20\\cdot d_1 = -20d_1$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $40\\cdot d_2 = 40d_2$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe **principle of moments** tells us that, for the two masses to balance, *the sum of the moments must equal zero*. \n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\n40d_2 - 20d_1 = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that the rod has length $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ we have\n\n$$\nd_1 + d_2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad d_2 = 1 - d_1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n40(1-d_1) - 20d_1 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich solves to give\n\n$$\nd_1 = \\dfrac23\\,\\textrm m.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if the fulcrum is placed at a distance of $\\dfrac23\\,\\textrm m$ from $A,$ the rod would balance perfectly. \n\nBefore we move on, note the following:\n\n- The fulcrum also has mass. However, since the distance between the fulcrum and itself is zero, its moment about the fulcrum is zero, and it doesn't feature when we apply the principle of moments. Thus, taking moments about the fulcrum was convenient because we don't need to know its mass! Generally, taking moments about a convenient point can save us a lot of work.\n\n- Selecting a positive sign for a clockwise moment and negative for a counterclockwise moment *may* seem counterintuitive. After all, positive angles are usually measured counterclockwise. However, we'll soon see that selecting the signs using this convention makes it easy to model moment problems using coordinate axes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130476 #edb/ref 1065 7805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130476 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "51c95b40-7a4e-52cb-b891-0d1a77fa6c7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130476 #edb/ref 1067 "The Principle of Moments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130477 #edb/ref 1064 "As discussed, we denote the direction of a moment as either clockwise or counterclockwise, depending on the direction in which it causes the rod to rotate about the fulcrum.\n\nIn general, the **total moment** about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={m}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{m}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+⋯+{m}_{n}{x}_{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total moment is a single number that indicates the combined turning effect of the system. A total moment of zero (as in the last example, taking the fulcrum as the origin) means no combined turning effect, so the system balances perfectly.\n\nThe moment of a single mass $m_i$ about the origin is equals $m_i x_i,$ and its sign is determined by its position along the $x$-axis: \n\n- If $x_i > 0,$ the induced motion is *clockwise*, and the associated moment is *positive*.\n\n- If $x_i < 0,$ the induced motion is *counterclockwise*, and the associated moment is *negative*.\n\nConsider the two point masses below, where the distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters. Let's find the total moment of this system about the origin.\n\n![](images/f3/f3521662d6cd15a6c12559b7b85e01943fc5d8c77de6b4b1ae06fb03d9533326.png)\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =5, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-10+12 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $2\\,\\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130477 #edb/ref 1065 7806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130477 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5b8573e7-76a8-56ee-93e9-6552ae020ec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130477 #edb/ref 1067 "Moments About the Origin" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130478 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a collection of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nThe system's **center of mass** is the point $\\bar{x}$ on the $x$-axis where the system's total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment as the original system. \n\n![](images/e2/e2d7dcb177010f0105d7887e14b84e52527381caa17ca01b91027db6d6cd40a7.png)\n\nLet's derive the equation for the center of mass $\\bar{x}$ for this system.\n\nThe total moment of the distributed masses about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\nm = m_1+m_2+\\cdots m_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i ,\n$$\n\nthen the total moment about the origin of the single mass $m$ located at $\\bar{x}$ equals\n\n$$\nm \\bar{x}.\n$$\n\nEquating our two moments, we get\n\n$$\nM_O = m \\bar{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\bar{x}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\bar{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe center of mass is an important physical quantity, as it gives the location where a fulcrum would need to be placed for the system to balance perfectly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130478 #edb/ref 1065 7807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130478 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8a03183c-37d2-59a1-9395-b7bcd95a21b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130478 #edb/ref 1067 "Center of Mass" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130479 #edb/ref 1064 "When a collection of masses is distributed in two or more dimensions of space, we must take moments about a fixed **axis.**\n\nIn the following work, we will only consider moments about the coordinate axes. \n\nConsider the collection of point masses below, where the distances along the $x$- and $y$-axes are measured in meters. \n\n![](images/32/32f9c207b835469dba729e3d5745dd1225eed6153aac4517e2c40e61b7edb126.png)\n\nThe center of mass of this two-dimensional system, denoted $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ is the point where the entire mass of the system could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the $x$- and $y$-axes as the original system. \n\nMore precisely, the center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ in the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's compute $\\bar{x},$ the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system. \n\nSince we require the [math]x[/math]-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the [math]y[/math]-axis.\n\nFirst, we denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of these masses are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -2, \\qquad x_2 = -1, \\qquad x_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $\\boldsymbol y$**-axis** is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6-5+16 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_y}{m} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130479 #edb/ref 1065 7808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130479 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3e42e688-f198-599c-8c1f-7a5852cad300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130479 #edb/ref 1067 "Center of Mass for Objects in the Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
